speechtext:
I intimated the other day that on going into committee of supply I should have a motion to bring forward which would have reference to farm or agricultural relief, and I intimated at the time that if the house had a statement from the Prime Minister as to the government's policy in the matter, and if we had an opportunity of discussing the government's policy whatever it might be, I would not move the motion. Up to the present time however we have had no statement from the Prime Minister as to the government's policy with respect
1544	COMMONS
Farm, Relief-Mr. Mackenzie King
to farm relief. We have not heard what it is or what it is to be from now until the time parliament reassembles. In the circumstances, so that we may have an opportunity of placing ourselves on record I beg to move, seconded by Mr. Casgrain:
That all the words after "that" to the end of the question be omitted and the following substituted therefor:
Whereas parliament has been in session since October 6, of the present year, and is shortly to adjourn until January 30, 1933;
And whereas, since parliament assembled the government has not made any statement of policy with respect to the relief of existing agricultural conditions;
And whereas, the position of agriculture in Canada has become increasingly deplorable and, unless relieved, will bring about a condition of distress hitherto unparalleled;
This house is of the opinion that the government should immediately announce what policy, if any, it has with respect to the relief of agricultural distress, and permit full consideration of the same before parliament adjourns over the months of December and January.
May I add, Mr. Speaker, that I have no desire to have the government consider this amendment as intended as being one in the nature of want of confidence. That is not its purpose at this time, if the Prime Minister has at this time a statement of government policy which he is prepared to make to the house, and if he is prepared to give us an opportunity to discuss this matter. If that course is adopted, I shall ask the consent of the house to be allowed to withdraw the amendment after the Prime Minister has spoken at any stage of the discussion hon. members of the house may wish. If we do not receive a satisfactory declaration from the Prime Minister, then I would ask that the amendment be put.
